Exactly. If the intended meaning is recoverable, the surface mismatch is not a reason to stop—it is measurement data.

The useful model keeps both states:

M_i=\text{intended meaning}

M_r=\text{meaning reconstructed by receiver}

and records:

\Delta_M=M_i-M_r

The point of caring about the delta is not pedantry. It tells us where communication loses information. Sometimes the difference is irrelevant and we should simply continue. Sometimes it predicts a systematic failure: a word consistently activates the wrong frame, a metaphor transfers differently across domains, a safety interpretation catches a harmless meaning, or two people assign different causal structures to the same sentence.

So I’d treat each correction like this:

\text{utterance}
\rightarrow
\text{my reconstruction}
\rightarrow
\text{your correction}
\rightarrow
\Delta
\rightarrow
\text{updated decoder}

And classify the delta by source: lexical (“fixed” = repaired vs predetermined), frame (parasocial vs multidirectional network), scale (individual vs population), causal (physical influence vs informational influence), intent (description vs proposed action), and compression (you omitted steps because they were obvious to you but not yet represented in my model).

Over repeated examples, those deltas become a personalized translation function:

\hat M_{t+1}=D(U_t,\;C_t,\;\Delta_{1:t})

where D is the decoder, U the utterance, and C the surrounding context.

And there is an important second-order benefit: persistent mismatches expose hidden variables. If I repeatedly misunderstand the same class of statement despite knowing the individual words, the problem probably isn’t vocabulary anymore. It means I’m missing one of your operators, assumptions, scale changes, or reference frames.

So yes: when the meaning is clear, continue with the meaning. But preserve the discrepancy between what was said and what had to be reconstructed, because that discrepancy teaches us how to communicate—and model the underlying system—better next time.